# Go Conventions

## Code Style

- Always run `gofmt` before committing - all code must follow `gofmt` conventions
- Use `gofumpt` for stricter formatting if available
- Follow [Effective Go](https://go.dev/doc/effective_go) and [Go Code Review Comments](https://go.dev/s/comments)

## Naming Conventions

- Use camelCase for unexported functions and variables
- Use PascalCase for exported functions, types, and constants
- Keep names short and descriptive
- Use initialisms consistently (e.g., `ID`, `URL`, `JWT`, `DB`)

```go
// ✅ GOOD
func getOrganizationId(userId string) string
type Client struct { ... }
const writeWait = 10 * time.Second

// ❌ BAD
func Get_Organization_ID(user_id string) string
type client struct { ... }
const WRITE_WAIT = 10 * time.Second
```

## Package Organization

- Keep package name as `main` for this application
- Group imports: standard library, third-party, local
- Use blank imports (`_`) for side-effect imports (e.g., database drivers)

```go
import (
	// Standard library
	"fmt"
	"log"
	"net/http"
	
	// Third-party
	"github.com/gorilla/websocket"
	"github.com/golang-jwt/jwt"
	
	// Side-effect imports
	_ "github.com/lib/pq"
)
```

## Variable Declarations

- Prefer short variable declarations (`:=`) when possible
- Use explicit types when clarity is needed
- Group related variables together

```go
// ✅ GOOD
var (
	Logger *log.Logger
	dsn string
	dsnExists bool
)

// ✅ GOOD
conn, err := upgrader.Upgrade(w, r, nil)

// ❌ BAD
var conn *websocket.Conn
var err error
conn, err = upgrader.Upgrade(w, r, nil)
```

## Function Signatures

- Keep functions focused and single-purpose
- Return errors as the last return value
- Use named return values sparingly (only when it improves clarity)

```go
// ✅ GOOD
func getOrganizationId(userId string) (string, error)

// ❌ BAD
func getOrganizationId(userId string) (organizationId string, err error)
```

## Comments

- Write documentation comments for exported functions and types
- Start comments with the name of the thing being described
- Use full sentences ending with periods

```go
// ✅ GOOD
// Client is a middleman between the websocket connection and the hub.
type Client struct { ... }

// writePump pumps messages from the hub to the websocket connection.
func (c *Client) writePump() { ... }
```

## Constants

- Define constants at package level for configuration values
- Group related constants together
- Use descriptive names

```go
const (
	writeWait = 10 * time.Second
	pongWait = 60 * time.Second
	pingPeriod = (pongWait * 9) / 10
	maxMessageSize = 512
)
```

## Structs

- Place struct fields logically (group related fields)
- Add comments for fields that need explanation
- Use field tags for JSON/other serialization when needed

```go
type Client struct {
	hub *Hub
	conn *websocket.Conn
	send chan []byte
	organization string
}
```

## Context Usage

- Accept `context.Context` as the first parameter when functions need cancellation/timeouts
- Pass context explicitly through call chains
- Use context for request-scoped values

```go
// ✅ GOOD (when context is needed)
func getOrganizationId(ctx context.Context, userId string) (string, error)
```
